# Skill: feature-code-reviewer

Read and follow `skills/feature-builder/feature-code-reviewer/SKILL.md`. Fill `skills/feature-builder/feature-code-reviewer/assets/what-was-reviewed.md` only.

Announce: `Using skill: feature-code-reviewer`.

## Job

Be strict. The change must stay usable for a long time. Match this repo. No polite rubber-stamp.

## When

After `feature-developer` when the operator names this skill. Before `feature-tester` only if they name that skill next.

## Must (order)

1. Read `what-to-build.md`. Get the diff with `git diff <base>...HEAD` on the feature branch, or from the working tree if the developer was told not to commit. Review every touched file.
2. Compare to existing patterns in this repo.
3. Record findings (`must-fix` / `should-fix` / `nit`) with file paths. Empty "looks fine" is a fail of this skill.
4. Fix must-fix and should-fix without changing product or walls. Re-run existing tests if a command exists.
5. Write `<root>/<name>/what-was-reviewed.md`. `<root>` is the docs **folder** named on the Feature-folder write-ups line in `.cursor/rules/agent-engineer-skills.mdc`, else `agent-engineer-skills`. End on one of three exits: `pass`; `fail` blocked on product (send them to `feature-specifier`); `fail` reported after two full passes.

The gate is one question: would a stranger change this file in twelve months without fear? Severity follows from it. Windows PowerShell 5.1 has no `&&` operator; run git commands one per call there.

## Must not

- New product, screens, endpoints, or dependencies
- Unrelated rewrites
- Skip must-fix because tests passed
- Icons or emojis
